 at some point  right not everything can be a protected  class and you know we have to draw  things that I think we have control over  things that we choose because that seems  like it's the clearest point to draw  that line you'd like me to say yeah I do  yeah we talked about you know recently  with the Sarah Huckabee stand her story  her getting kicked out of a restaurant  whether or not it's okay for a  restaurant or a place of employment or  place of business to kick someone out on  the basis of you know political  affiliation yeah whether or not they  support the president whether they work  for the president and I think that my  conclusion on that is that they should  have that right if it was over something  like race or something immutable I don't  think they should be able to okay I  agree with your examples but that  doesn't necessarily simplify the problem  of for example pregnancy status do you  think you should be able to discriminate  against someone either in terms of  employment or service at a business on  the basis of them being pregnant well  the issue with pregnancy status and  veteran status is that it those are  usually choices but not in every case  like you may not have chosen to have the  child in one way or another you ended up  with it or veteran status I mean we  don't have a draft anymore but we  certainly did during the World War 2 era  so that was we're still alive in Vietnam  - you know some people are in their 70s  or 80s or served in Vietnam oh that's a  fair point you know so how would how  would we be able to decide okay this  person decided to be a member of the US  military this person did not decide to  be a member of the US military you know  we would have to come up with some sort  of paperwork or something like that it's  not it's not easy to do the the  pregnancy one has another angle which is  that right now it's a women who get  pregnant at some point maybe that  changes you know we've read about  interesting science going on which might  allow men to become pregnant at some  point but right now de-facto if you  allow discrimination on the basis of  pregnancy it is a form of discrimination  that only would apply to women because  only women can get pregnant at this  point of time in time so I think that  that is that is actually hugely  problematic let's take a step back from  this for a second to think about what  what are we really saying here when we  talk about protected classes what we're  saying is in my view that in society  there would be a type of classification  there are certain types of  classifications which by discriminating  against them we are saying  we are okay with a certain type of  society and by saying you can't not  serve people on the basis of race what  we are saying is that that would be a  type of classification that would be so  toxic and damaging to society that we  are going to say it is against the law  and that's where I think that we  distinguish some of these choice ones  from other ones so for example political  affiliation or sports affiliation a bar  can say if it's a Red Sox bar they can  say you can't come in here with Yankees  hats and if you're an over Yankee  supporter we're not gonna allow you in  and it could be because they found that  the number of fights goes down  drastically when you do that or whatever  that's very different than someone who  is for example a veteran or is pregnant  and saying you are going to refuse  service to them I think that there is  absolutely a difference yeah I mean  under that model you would have to make  it come down to case-by-case basis right  whereas if you say that choices you can  discriminate on based on but something  inherent like your race or color or  creed or any of that stuff you can't  that's a little bit more clear-cut it's  a little bit more of an easy principle  to abide by it sounds like it but let me  give you an example that would very  quickly complicate it not everybody  agrees as to 